# Copy to uc.env and fill in. uc.env holds real secrets and is gitignored — never commit it.
#
#   cp uc.env.example uc.env && edit uc.env
#   ./deploy-uc.sh

# --- Persistent root ---
# Point this at a persistent (cloud-disk) path. server.properties, hibernate.properties,
# relyt_jwks.json and the H2 metastore DB all live under it, so nothing is lost on restart.
# Default if unset: the UC install root (one level up from deploy/).
UC_HOME=

# --- Listen port ---
# Port the UC server listens on. 8088 is what the phoenix/presto e2e suites expect; change it only
# if you have a conflict. An explicit --port passed to deploy-uc.sh wins.
UC_PORT=8088

# --- Auth / token-exchange ---
# enable = require auth (per-user token-exchange); disable = no auth.
UC_AUTHORIZATION=enable
# OPTIONAL extra trusted issuers, comma-separated. Trust is derived primarily from the JWKS file
# (each key's "issuer" member, hot-reloaded); this list is a UNION on top, only for issuers NOT in
# the local JWKS (e.g. OIDC discovery). Leave EMPTY to be governed entirely by the JWKS -- the
# recommended default for Relyt (onboard a DWSU by appending its key to the JWKS: no restart, no
# change here).
UC_ALLOWED_ISSUERS=
# Expected audience of the subject tokens (must match the signer's unity.audience).
UC_AUDIENCES=unitycatalog-server
# Exchanged access-token TTL (ISO-8601). The server treats a blank value as "never expires";
# this sample ships PT12H (12 hours) on purpose — keep it unless you have a reason to opt out.
UC_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=PT12H

# --- UI sign-in (issue #15) ---
# The UI requires sign-in. Two entry points, both decided by these settings at runtime (no UI
# rebuild): "Sign in with Microsoft" on the login page, and the administrator password at
# <ui>/login/admin (not linked from the login page).

# Administrator password. Blank = administrator sign-in off. Keep it strong: it is compared in
# constant time but not rate-limited beyond a short delay per failure.
UC_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Microsoft Entra ID sign-in, optional. Leave all four blank to offer only the administrator
# sign-in. To enable, register an application in Entra (redirect URI =
# <browser-facing UC URL>/api/1.0/unity-control/auth/callback), then:
#   - configure the "email" optional claim, or accounts cannot be matched to UC users
#   - create each employee in UC (POST /scim2/Users) with their Microsoft email; grants decide
#     which catalogs / schemas / tables they see
# deploy-uc.sh derives what the token check needs from these four and prints each addition: the
# client id joins UC_AUDIENCES (an id_token is addressed to the application), and the issuer joins
# UC_ALLOWED_ISSUERS when the authorization URL names a tenant GUID on the v2.0 endpoint. Anything
# else -- a v1.0 endpoint, a tenant named by domain, common/organizations -- issues under a
# different name, so the script says so and leaves the issuer to you.
#
# Shape of the four values:
#   UC_AUTHORIZATION_URL=https://login.microsoftonline.com/TENANTID/oauth2/v2.0/authorize
#   UC_TOKEN_URL=https://login.microsoftonline.com/TENANTID/oauth2/v2.0/token
#   UC_CLIENT_ID=CLIENTID
#   UC_CLIENT_SECRET=CLIENTSECRET
# TENANTID and CLIENTID are both GUIDs but name different things -- never paste one for the other:
#   TENANTID      the customer's Entra tenant, i.e. the organization. Appears only inside the two
#                 URLs above. Public: read it, and both endpoints, off the customer's email domain
#                 without signing in -- authorization_endpoint, token_endpoint and the GUID inside
#                 issuer, from
#                   curl -s https://login.microsoftonline.com/<their-email-domain>/v2.0/.well-known/openid-configuration
#                 It must be that GUID: a domain name in its place still signs people in, but stops
#                 the issuer from being derived (see above).
#   CLIENTID      the application registered inside that tenant -- "Application (client) ID" in the
#                 portal. One tenant holds many; it exists only once someone registers the app. The
#                 id_token is addressed to it, which is why deploy-uc.sh appends it to UC_AUDIENCES.
#   CLIENTSECRET  that same application's secret VALUE, shown once at creation -- not the Secret ID
#                 displayed next to it.
#
# Leave the four below empty to keep Microsoft sign-in off. Setting only some of them does not
# half-enable it: deploy-uc.sh warns and the flow stays off until all four are set.
UC_AUTHORIZATION_URL=
UC_TOKEN_URL=
UC_CLIENT_ID=
UC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Browser-facing base URL of UC (scheme://host[:port], no path). It builds the OAuth redirect_uri
# and decides whether the session cookie is marked Secure. NORMALLY LEAVE BLANK: the server derives
# the browser-facing origin from X-Forwarded-Proto/Host, which the deployment puts there. Set it
# only when those headers do not reach the server -- a proxy that strips them, or a TLS terminator
# that never adds them. Two ways to get it wrong, both silent:
#   - a scheme that does not match reality: https here over plain HTTP makes the browser drop the
#     session cookie (sign-in appears to succeed and lands back on the login page); http here over
#     HTTPS leaves the cookie without Secure
#   - anything after the host: the redirect_uri no longer equals the one registered in Entra, which
#     answers AADSTS50011 -- deploy-uc.sh rejects a path outright
#   UC_EXTERNAL_URL=https://uc.example.com
UC_EXTERNAL_URL=

# --- Aliyun OSS (master RAM identity for STS AssumeRole) ---
ALIYUN_REGION=cn-hangzhou
ALIYUN_ACCESS_KEY=<your-aliyun-access-key-id>
ALIYUN_SECRET_KEY=<your-aliyun-access-key-secret>
# The master RAM principal the UC server runs as. Despite the ROLE in the variable name, this is
# normally the RAM *user* ARN (acs:ram::<account-id>:user/<name>) — the identity that calls
# AssumeRole — NOT the per-location role that gets assumed.
ALIYUN_MASTER_ROLE_ARN=acs:ram::<account-id>:user/<master-ram-user>

# --- Path overrides (optional) ---
# These two are genuinely relocatable: their paths are recorded as values inside the rendered
# config, so the server reads them wherever you point them.
# UC_EXTERNAL_JWKS_FILE=$UC_HOME/etc/conf/relyt_jwks.json
# UC_DB_FILE=$UC_HOME/etc/db/h2db
#
# These two are NOT. The server opens "etc/conf/server.properties" and "etc/conf/hibernate.properties"
# relative to its working directory, which is UC_HOME — the paths are compiled in, not configurable.
# The variables below only move where deploy-uc.sh WRITES the rendered files, so setting them
# renders the config somewhere the server never looks and it starts with the old one. Move UC_HOME
# instead; they are listed here only to say why not to use them.
# UC_SERVER_PROPERTIES=$UC_HOME/etc/conf/server.properties
# UC_HIBERNATE_PROPERTIES=$UC_HOME/etc/conf/hibernate.properties
